Dialogue: 0,0:00:02.17,0:00:05.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An aside: the five statements\Nbehind the pictures.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.85,0:00:09.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each is elementary; what\Nmatters is its exact scope.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.68,0:00:12.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first is almost too simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.25,0:00:19.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Suppose every field a {\c&HF09CB4&}decoder{\c&HF7F4F2&} can draw\Nobeys a linear law, like zero divergence.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.41,0:00:24.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then every sample obeys it, whatever the\N{\c&HF09CB4&}crowd of codes{\c&HF7F4F2&}, however badly trained.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.38,0:00:27.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The proof is one line.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.46,0:00:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The work is in the decoder:\Nhill maps in two dimensions,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.30,0:00:35.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a vector potential in three,\Nwith its gauge fixed.
